From 946c6baede9485784d574c9c77b21b3332cc8445 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: ayiejosh <132535860+ayiejosh@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Fri, 16 Oct 2026 07:48:03 +0800
Subject: [PATCH 115/269] gallium/pvr: pipe_resource over buffer objects and
 device addresses

The driver can now hand out resources: allocate a buffer object, give it a device
address, map it into the GPU's address space, and destroy it in the right order.

The screen owns the GPU address space, created at screen creation. Failure to get
it is deliberately NOT fatal to enumeration - it only means resources cannot be
created, which is worth being able to see when probing a node this driver cannot
actually drive.

ponytail ceiling, stated in the header: v1 lays every resource out linearly and
supports only what that honestly describes - buffers and non-mipmapped,
non-arrayed 2D textures. The layout the GPU needs for texturing (tiling, and the
stride and padding rules that go with it) is stage 3. Until then a resource is real
memory the GPU can reach, not yet something a sampler can read. Mipmaps, arrays,
multisampling and 3D are refused rather than accepted and laid out as if they were
level 0 - the failure that matters is the quiet one.

Verified on the real open kernel module, ALL PASS:

  resource: 256x256 RGBA8 -> stride 1024, 262144 bytes, device address 0x9ffffc0000
  resource: PASS (create, device address, GPU map, destroy; mipmaps refused)

The check also confirms a mipmapped resource is refused. Incidental validation:
creating a second VM context on the same fd (one from the screen, one from the VM
check) works, so multiple contexts per file are fine.
